.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "run-parallel [![travis][travis-image]][travis-url] [![npm][npm-image]][npm-url] [![downloads][downloads-image]][downloads-url] [![javascript style guide][standard-image]][standard-url]"
.PP
.SS "Run an array of functions in parallel"
 \fR\fP
.SS "install"
.PP
.nf
npm install run\-parallel
.fi
.PP
.SS "usage"
.SS "parallel(tasks, [callback])"
Run the \fRtasks\fP array of functions in parallel, without waiting until the previous function has completed\&. If any of the functions pass an error to its callback, the main \fRcallback\fP is immediately called with the value of the error\&. Once the \fRtasks\fP have completed, the results are passed to the final \fRcallback\fP as an array\&.

.PP
It is also possible to use an object instead of an array\&. Each property will be run as a function and the results will be passed to the final \fRcallback\fP as an object instead of an array\&. This can be a more readable way of handling the results\&.
.SS "arguments"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtasks\fP - An array or object containing functions to run\&. Each function is passed a \fRcallback(err, result)\fP which it must call on completion with an error \fRerr\fP (which can be \fRnull\fP) and an optional \fRresult\fP value\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcallback(err, results)\fP - An optional callback to run once all the functions have completed\&. This function gets a results array (or object) containing all the result arguments passed to the task callbacks\&.
.PP
.SS "example"
.PP
.nf
var parallel = require('run\-parallel')

parallel([
  function (callback) {
    setTimeout(function () {
      callback(null, 'one')
    }, 200)
  },
  function (callback) {
    setTimeout(function () {
      callback(null, 'two')
    }, 100)
  }
],
// optional callback
function (err, results) {
  // the results array will equal ['one','two'] even though
  // the second function had a shorter timeout\&.
})
.fi
.PP

.PP
This module is basically equavalent to \fR\fRasync\&.parallel\fP\fP, but it's handy to just have the one function you need instead of the kitchen sink\&. Modularity! Especially handy if you're serving to the browser and need to reduce your javascript bundle size\&.

.PP
Works great in the browser with \fRbrowserify\fP!
